This is a production-grade implementation. Since you are at TCS and looking toward companies like Amazon, this structure shows you understand Separation of Concerns, DTOs, and Spring-managed Factories.
1. The Model (DTO)
We use a Record (or a simple class) to transfer data. This keeps the data separate from the logic.
// The request coming from your Controller (React Frontend)
public record PaymentRequest(
    String bankName, // e.g., "HDFC", "SBI"
    double amount,
    String accountNo
) {}

2. The Strategy Interface
This is the "Contract" that all banks must follow.
public interface PaymentProcessor {
    void process(PaymentRequest request);
    String getBankName(); // The "Key" for our Factory Map
}

3. Concrete Services (The "Workers")
Each bank gets its own class. This makes the code highly maintainable.
@Service
public class HdfcProcessor implements PaymentProcessor {
    @Override
    public void process(PaymentRequest request) {
        System.out.println("Processing HDFC Payment of ₹" + request.amount() + " for Acc: " + request.accountNo());
    }

    @Override
    public String getBankName() {
        return "HDFC";
    }
}

@Service
public class SbiProcessor implements PaymentProcessor {
    @Override
    public void process(PaymentRequest request) {
        System.out.println("Processing SBI Payment of ₹" + request.amount() + " for Acc: " + request.accountNo());
    }

    @Override
    public String getBankName() {
        return "SBI";
    }
}

4. The Factory (The "Manager")
This is the code we discussed. It "loads" the services at startup.
@Component
public class PaymentFactory {

    private final Map<String, PaymentProcessor> processorMap;

    // Spring finds all PaymentProcessor beans and injects them here
    @Autowired
    public PaymentFactory(List<PaymentProcessor> processors) {
        this.processorMap = new HashMap<>();
        for (PaymentProcessor p : processors) {
            processorMap.put(p.getBankName().toUpperCase(), p);
        }
    }

    public PaymentProcessor get(String bankName) {
        PaymentProcessor processor = processorMap.get(bankName.toUpperCase());
        if (processor == null) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Bank not supported: " + bankName);
        }
        return processor;
    }
}

5. The Controller (The "Entry Point")
This is where your React frontend will hit the API.
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/payments")
public class PaymentController {

    private final PaymentFactory paymentFactory;

    // Constructor Injection (Best Practice)
    public PaymentController(PaymentFactory paymentFactory) {
        this.paymentFactory = paymentFactory;
    }

    @PostMapping("/execute")
    public String pay(@RequestBody PaymentRequest request) {
        // 1. Ask the Factory for the right processor
        PaymentProcessor processor = paymentFactory.get(request.bankName());
        
        // 2. Execute the payment logic
        processor.process(request);
        
        return "Payment processed successfully via " + request.bankName();
    }
}

Why this code makes you look like a Senior Lead:
 * No new keywords: Everything is managed by Spring's IoC container.
 * No switch or if-else: The PaymentController doesn't care if you have 2 banks or 200 banks.
 * Immutability: By using private final and constructor injection, your code is thread-safe—perfect for high-concurrency systems.
 * Extensibility: If TCS asks you to add "Axis Bank" tomorrow, you don't touch the Controller or the Factory. You just add one new Service class.
